course: everyday
lesson: 394
title: Making peace between the streets
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: For as long as anyone could remember, Maddie's street and Station Road had been at war — over the rec, the corner shop, the best tree, and mostly over who had started it, which nobody could actually remember. Then Mr Pryce had an idea: a football match, but with mixed teams, half from each street on each side. For the first ten minutes nobody passed to anyone from the other street. Then Kieran missed an open goal so badly that both streets laughed together, and after that the ball started going everywhere.
explain: Peace is built from lots of small things, not one big speech. Maddie and her swimming friend from Station Road organised a picnic for both streets, with crisps, a bubble machine and a very sticky cake. Sam, who hates shouting, suggested a joint art project: a long mural on the underpass wall, painted by both streets, with a fox from each end meeting in the middle. Lily invited girls from both streets to her birthday, and her mum had to buy twice as many party bags.
explain: The hardest parts were the honest ones. Kieran and Jake shook hands and agreed: no more street fights, and if someone starts one, they walk away and tell. Maddie and Kieran went to a Station Road boy they had hurt in a scrap the summer before and said a real sorry, without «but you started it». He shrugged, said «yeah, all right», and later he passed Maddie the ball. Saying sorry first felt worse than any bruise, Maddie said, and better afterwards.
explain: Mr Pryce set up a «peace bench» in the playground: if two kids fell out, they sat on it together until they had talked it through. And because the rec was still too small for everyone, the two streets agreed to share it — Station Road on Tuesdays and Thursdays, Maddie's street on Mondays and Wednesdays, and everyone together on Saturdays for football with the police officer. The parents of both streets even ran a summer fete together, and argued only about the tombola.
fact: Long fights between groups often go on long after anyone remembers how they started.
fact: Mixed teams make people work with the «other side» and get to know them as people.
fact: Laughing together and shared fun help groups stop seeing each other as enemies.
fact: Peace is usually built from many small steps, like games, parties and shared projects.
fact: A real apology names what you did and does not add «but you started it».
fact: Apologising first can feel hard, but it often makes the other person ready to make peace.
fact: Agreeing clear rules, such as «walk away and tell», helps a truce last.
fact: Sharing a place fairly, for example by taking turns on different days, can end fights about who owns it.
fact: A «peace bench» or friendship bench is a set place where children go to talk a quarrel through.
fact: When adults from both sides work together, children see that the two sides can get on.
exercise: What were the two streets fighting about? | The rec, the shop, the best tree and who started it | A stolen bike from last summer and nothing else at all | Which street had the bigger school | Who had the best football team only
exercise: What did nobody remember? | Who had actually started the fighting | Where exactly the rec was and who owned it | The real names of the two streets on the map | How to play football properly, with rules
exercise: What was special about the football match? | Each team had kids from both streets | Each street had its own team with its own captain and kit | Only girls could play | There was no referee
exercise: What happened in the first ten minutes of the match? | Nobody passed to the other street's players | Everybody became friends straight away, from the first whistle | A fight broke out | The ball went missing
exercise: What changed the match? | Kieran's terrible miss made both streets laugh together | A teacher came out and shouted at them all to stop arguing | It started raining | Someone scored an own goal on purpose
exercise: What did Maddie and her swimming friend organise? | A picnic for both streets | A race between the two streets | A fight with proper rules and a ref | A trip to the swimming pool
exercise: What was Sam's idea? | A mural painted by both streets together | A tall new wall built to keep the two streets apart | A shouting contest | A new gang called the Foxes
exercise: Why did the mural have a fox from each end meeting in the middle? | To show the two streets coming together | Because lots of real foxes live on the rec at night | Because a fox bit somebody | To mark the border between streets
exercise: What did Lily do to help? | Invited girls from both streets to her party | Only invited the girls from her own street, as usual | Cancelled her party | Gave out presents to the boys
exercise: What did Kieran and Jake agree? | No more street fights; walk away and tell | Fight, but only on Saturdays | Get their big brothers to do the fighting instead | Never speak to each other again
exercise: What made Maddie and Kieran's apology a real one? | They said sorry without «but you started it» | They said sorry and then blamed him for the whole thing | They sent a text only | They made him say sorry first
exercise: How did Maddie feel about saying sorry first? | Worse than a bruise, then better afterwards | Easy, and not really worth mentioning to anyone | Angry the whole time | Embarrassed for ever
exercise: What is the peace bench for? | Two kids who fell out sit and talk it through | Where kids who are being punished have to sit at break | Sitting alone when you're sad | Teachers' tea breaks
exercise: How did the streets share the rec? | Different days for each street, Saturdays together | Station Road got it all to themselves, every single day | Nobody used it any more | Whoever got there first kept it
exercise: What did the parents of both streets do? | Ran a summer fete together | Built a fence between the streets | Told the kids to stay inside all summer | Moved all the families to another town
exercise: What did the parents still argue about? | The tombola | The football match | Which street started the fights | Who owned the rec
test: «we've ALWAYS hated Station Road». why tho? | Nobody even remembers how it started | Because of a strict rule made by the council | Because they are worse people than us | Because their school is bigger and newer
test: coach: «mixed teams today, half each street». Kieran: «no way». Why's it a good idea? | Playing together turns enemies into teammates | It makes the game much shorter and less tiring for everyone | It stops anyone scoring | It keeps the two streets apart
test: Kieran blasts it over the bar from 2 metres, both streets in stitches. Why does that matter? | Laughing together breaks the ice | It means Kieran is rubbish at football | It starts a new fight | It ends the game
test: «sorry… but u started it». Is that a real apology? | No, a real sorry doesn't blame the other person | Yes, it has the word sorry in it, so it counts | Yes, if he did start it | Only if you say it twice
test: Maddie, red-faced: «I'm sorry I hurt you last summer». What happens most often after a real sorry? | The other person is more ready to make peace | The other person hits you as soon as you say it | Nothing ever changes | Everyone laughs at you
test: Jake and a Station Road lad fall out at break. Teacher points at the peace bench. What do they do? | Sit together and talk it through | Sit apart and sulk until the bell | Fight next to the bench where nobody sees | Take turns sitting alone on the bench
test: «the rec is OURS». «no it's OURS». What's a fair fix? | Take turns on different days and share Saturdays | Fight for it once and for all, and the winner keeps it | Let the biggest kids have it | Close it so nobody gets it
test: Lily's mum: «twice as many party bags?!» Why did Lily invite both streets? | To help the girls from both streets be friends | To get more presents from more people | Because her mum told her she had to | Because she lost the list of who to invite
test: Kieran: «a picnic won't stop anything». What's true? | Peace is built from lots of small things like that | Only a big speech in assembly can stop fights like that | Only adults can make peace | Picnics make fights worse
test: «mural?? art's for babies». Sam's reply that shows why it helps? | «Painting it together means we all own it» | «You're right, let's not bother then» | «Only my street can paint it, not theirs» | «It's to keep them out»
test: Kieran and Jake shook on «no more fights». What makes the deal last? | A clear plan: walk away and tell | Shaking hands harder than before | Never seeing each other again, not even at school | Telling nobody about it
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the composition of mixed teams from two rival streets may diminish hostility between them. | Co-operation towards a common aim fosters acquaintance between members of the two groups | Mixed teams prevent any player from scoring | Mixed teams remove the need for adult supervision | Hostility is diminished solely by the fatigue that follows vigorous exercise
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a genuine apology? | It acknowledges the wrong committed without attributing blame to the injured party | It is valid only where the injured party apologises first | It requires the qualification «but you started it», so that the truth is recorded | It consists solely in the utterance of the word «sorry»
exam: It is given that two communities dispute the use of a single recreation ground. Select the arrangement most consistent with a fair and durable settlement. | The allocation of separate days to each, with shared use on an agreed day | The award of exclusive use to the more numerous community | The resolution of the dispute by a single contest of strength between the two streets | The permanent closure of the recreation ground
exam: State the conclusion consistent with the established facts concerning the restoration of peace between rival groups. | Peace is customarily established through numerous modest joint undertakings | Peace is established solely through a single formal declaration | Peace is established only when one group withdraws entirely | Peace is unattainable once hostilities have commenced
